## 147. Mental Model

Imagine the diagram is made of LEGO pieces.

```text
SMALL PIECES
     ↓
COMBINE
     ↓
MEDIUM FIGURES
     ↓
COMBINE AGAIN
     ↓
LARGE FIGURES
```

Your job is to count every valid figure produced at every level.

---

## 148. Ultimate Rule

> **Never count what you see only. Count what the lines can form.**

That is the central principle of figure-counting reasoning.

---

## 149. Final Revision Map

```text
                 COUNTING OF FIGURES
                         │
        ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐
        │                │                │
      LINES            GRIDS           COMPLEX
        │                │                │
     Segments         Squares         Triangles
     Intersections    Rectangles      Quadrilaterals
     Angles           Regions         Polygons
        │                │             Composite
        │                │             Overlapping
        └────────────────┼────────────────┘
                         │
                         ▼
                SIZE + ORIENTATION
                         │
                         ▼
                    VALIDATE
                         │
                         ▼
                   DEDUPLICATE
                         │
                         ▼
                       TOTAL
```

---

## 150. One-Line Formula Memory

```text
n points → segments = nC2

n×n grid → squares = 1²+2²+...+n²

m×n grid → rectangles = C(m+1,2)×C(n+1,2)

n-gon → diagonals = n(n−3)/2

n general lines → max intersections = nC2

n general lines → max regions = n(n+1)/2 + 1
```

